{:edb/db-before-t 1181 :edb/db-after-t 1182 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534494 50 #inst "2026-10-07T08:55:20.992Z" 13194139534494 true] [17592186156926 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$" 13194139534494 true] [17592186156926 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x) = x \\sin{x}\n$$\n\nis an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-x) &= (-x)\\sin (-x) \\\\\n&=-x(-\\sin x) \\\\\n&= x\\sin x \\\\\n&= f(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because\n\n$$\nf(x) = x \\sin{x}\n$$\n\nis an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx\n$$" 13194139534494 false] [17592186156927 1100 "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" 13194139534494 true] [17592186156927 1100 "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" 13194139534494 false] [17592186156927 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534494 true] [17592186156927 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n$f(-x)|= 4(-x)^{3}; =-4x^{3}; =-f(x)✓$\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n$\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0$.\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n$g(-x)|= e^{(-x)} + e^{- (-x)}; = e^{-x} + e^{x}; = e^{x} + e^{-x}; \\ne - g(x)\\cdot$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534494 false] [17592186156928 1100 "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A$, what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx$?" 13194139534494 false] [17592186156928 1100 "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" 13194139534494 true] [17592186156928 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534494 true] [17592186156928 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x) = x\\cos x\n$$\n\nis an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-x) &= (-x)\\cos (-x) \\\\\n&=-x\\cos x \\\\\n&= - (x\\cos x) \\\\\n&=-f(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because\n\n$$\nf(x) = x\\cos x\n$$\n\nis an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{1}x\\cos xdx &= 0 \\\\\n∫_{-1}^{0}x\\cos xdx + ∫_{0}^{1}x\\cos xdx &= 0 \\\\\n∫_{-1}^{0}x\\cos xdx + A &= 0 \\\\\n∫_{-1}^{0}x\\cos xdx &= -A\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534494 false] [17592186157327 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291806 13194139534494 true] [17592186157327 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291813 13194139534494 true] [17592186157327 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291855 13194139534494 true] [17592186157327 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291862 13194139534494 true] [17592186157328 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291820 13194139534494 true] [17592186157328 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291848 13194139534494 true] [17592186157328 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291869 13194139534494 true] [17592186157328 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291876 13194139534494 true] [17592186157329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291827 13194139534494 true] [17592186157329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291834 13194139534494 true] [17592186157329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291838 13194139534494 true] [17592186157329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291841 13194139534494 true] [17592186157329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291844 13194139534494 true] [17592186157891 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534494 true] [17592186157891 1100 "${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139534494 true] [17592186157891 1100 "What is $∫_{-3}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx$?" 13194139534494 false] [17592186157891 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139534494 true] [17592186157893 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534494 true] [17592186157893 1100 "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139534494 true] [17592186157893 1100 "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n1. $∫_{-2}^{2}3x^{2}dx$\n2. $∫_{-3}^{6}5x^{3}dx$\n3. $∫_{-9}^{9}2x^{5}dx$" 13194139534494 false] [17592186157893 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=5{x}^{3}$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534494 true] [17592186291806 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291807 13194139534494 true] [17592186291806 1089 "q-115256" 13194139534494 true] [17592186291806 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534494 true] [17592186291806 1100 "${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139534494 true] [17592186291806 1113 #uuid "bbb96456-68ae-5b30-9f53-6b93317d471e" 13194139534494 true] [17592186291806 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-x)^{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6}) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139534494 true] [17592186291807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291808 13194139534494 true] [17592186291807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291809 13194139534494 true] [1759218629