1848 1089 "q-88208" 13194139534494 true] [17592186291848 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534494 true] [17592186291848 1100 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139534494 true] [17592186291848 1113 #uuid "f8db7c7d-2c36-54ae-a136-cfed51754274" 13194139534494 true] [17592186291848 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x+1) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-x) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2x \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534494 true] [17592186291849 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291850 13194139534494 true] [17592186291849 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291851 1319