534494 true] [17592186291869 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534494 true] [17592186291870 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291871 13194139534494 true] [17592186291870 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291872 13194139534494 true] [17592186291870 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291873 13194139534494 true] [17592186291870 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291874 13194139534494 true] [17592186291870 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291875 13194139534494 true] [17592186291870 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534494 true] [17592186291870 1041 "selection" 13194139534494 true] [17592186291870 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291873 13194139534494 true] [17592186291870 1110 #uuid "c8acd680-1834-52d3-a1e2-a9009635a9d4" 13194139534494 true] [17592186291871 1034 "II and III only" 131941395344